            answer="Subject matter jurisdiction is the power of a court to hear a particular type of case, like federal laws or disputes between citizens of different states. Federal question jurisdiction (28 USC 1331) lets federal courts handle cases involving the U.S. Constitution or federal laws, while diversity jurisdiction allows them to hear state law disputes when parties are from different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. Complete diversity requires no overlap in state citizenship between all plaintiffs and defendants. For example, a New York resident suing a Texas company for $100,000 in contract damages might go to federal court if there’s complete diversity and the amount meets the threshold.",
