            answer="The harmless error standard is a legal principle that allows appellate courts to uphold a trial court's decision even if there was an error, as long as the error did not affect the defendant's substantial rights. Harmless error occurs when a trial court makes a mistake, but it doesn't prejudice the defendant's case or change the outcome, while a prejudicial error is one that does affect the defendant's rights. A substantial right refers to a legal right that is essential to a fair trial. Under FRCP 61, appellate courts should disregard any error, defect, irregularity, or variance that does not affect a substantial right. For example, if a judge incorrectly allows a witness to testify but the testimony was not crucial to the case, it may be considered harmless error and not a reversible error.",
