            answer="The law of the case doctrine requires courts to follow their own prior rulings in the same case, ensuring consistency, while stare decisis requires courts to follow their own past decisions in different cases as binding precedent. Law of the case applies within the same proceeding, binding lower courts on remand to adhere to appellate rulings unless there’s a clear error, whereas stare decisis binds courts generally to follow prior decisions across cases. For example, if an appellate court decides a contract issue in a case, the trial court must follow that ruling on remand, but stare decisis would require any future court to follow that same contract rule in other cases.",
