            answer="Murder requires malice aforethought, meaning intent to kill or extreme recklessness, while manslaughter lacks this malice. Murder can involve premeditation or felony murder, whereas manslaughter includes voluntary (heat-of-passion killings after provocation) or involuntary (unintentional deaths due to negligence or felony actions) forms. For example, if someone kills a spouse during a sudden argument triggered by betrayal, it might be voluntary manslaughter, but if the death resulted from a reckless, unplanned action without provocation, it could be involuntary manslaughter.",
