            answer="The insanity defense claims a defendant was mentally ill at the time of the crime and couldn't understand their actions or distinguish right from wrong, affecting their guilt. Incompetency to stand trial refers to whether the defendant can currently understand the proceedings or assist their lawyer, not their guilt. The M'Naghten rule requires the defendant didn't know their actions were wrong; the irresistible impulse test focuses on inability to control actions despite knowing they're wrong; the Model Penal Code test combines both knowledge and control; the Durham test asks if the crime was caused by mental illness. Incompetency involves understanding the trial, while 'guilty but mentally ill' means the defendant is found guilty but needs treatment. For example, a person with schizophrenia who couldn’t recognize their actions as criminal at the time might be found insane, while someone who can’t follow trial rules might be deemed incompetent.",
