            answer="The key difference between a joint tenancy and a tenancy in common is the presence of the right of survivorship in joint tenancy. In a joint tenancy, when one owner dies, their interest automatically passes to the other owners, while in a tenancy in common, each owner's interest can be inherited by their heirs. A joint tenancy requires the four unities of time, title, interest, and possession to create the right of survivorship, whereas a tenancy in common does not require these unities and allows for separate ownership interests. For example, if two siblings own a house as joint tenants, when one dies, the other automatically gets the full ownership, but if they own it as tenants in common, the deceased sibling's share would go to their heirs instead.",
