            answer="The 'relation back' doctrine in property law means that a recorded property interest is treated as if it existed from the moment the transaction occurred, not when it was recorded, affecting priority between recorded and unrecorded interests. A recording act is a law requiring property transfers to be recorded to protect buyers; race statutes prioritize the first to record, notice statutes prioritize those who record and provide notice, and race-notice statutes combine both. A bona fide purchaser (someone buying in good faith) may lose priority if they don’t record promptly. For example, if Alice buys land and records the deed, then Bob buys the same land without recording, Alice’s interest takes priority because it ‘relates back’ to her earlier transaction, even if Bob’s purchase was later.",
