            answer="When an officer makes a lawful arrest based on probable cause, the officer may search the arrestee's person and the area within the arrestee's immediate control — the 'wingspan' — to protect officer safety and prevent the destruction of evidence. Under the Chimel rule, the search may not extend beyond that area without another justification. The rule also lets officers search the arrestee's pockets and remove containers found on the person, and they may examine their contents. For example, an officer who arrests a driver at the roadside may search the driver's jacket and the car's glove compartment if it is within reach, but may not open the trunk without a warrant or another exception.",
