            answer="Under Rule 4.1, a lawyer must not make a false statement of material fact or law to a third person in the course of a representation. Rule 4.4 requires the lawyer to respect the rights of third parties and refrain from tactics with no substantial purpose other than to embarrass, delay, or burden another person. The lawyer must not induce a witness to hide or destroy evidence, and must not instruct a client to do so. For example, a lawyer negotiating a settlement may not falsely claim another offer is on the table, and must not send a private investigator to secretly record an opposing witness in violation of local law.",
